ZERO NATIVE SOURCE COMPOSITION

tools/integrate.zero replaces the former foreign-language source insertion
scripts. It is original Zero, built as a native Linux/Windows command.

  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_SOURCE.zero [--ezero|--picture|--picture-check|--browser|--browser-check]

Default mode reads kernel.zero as the complete source template. It replaces
the filesystem, window, network, guest compiler, guest file-service and Zero
text-reader, browser, mouse and guest-editor marker
blocks with their original Zero source fragments. The allowed NET, APP,
BROWSER, MOUSE and EDITOR
include lines expand recursively from a fixed filename whitelist. Network
client receive/poll markers expand from their own source parts. Required
markers must occur exactly once; missing/duplicate blocks, missing closing
markers, unknown includes, NUL source bytes and excessive bounds fail before
an output is created. No arbitrary include pathname is executed or imported.

--ezero concatenates head, lexer, parser, expression and finish original Zero
frontend parts in that order. This regenerates the complete Ezero compiler
source; it does not implement Ezero parsing in another language.

--picture replaces the shared memory-only ZPIX decoder block in tools/picture.zero.
--picture-check replaces the same block in media/picture-check.zero. The decoder
marker is required exactly once in these modes; both preserve the surrounding
original Zero source. See media/ZPIX.ztext for the memory contract.

--browser concatenates the browser reader head, HTML declarations, reader
input, HTML parser and reader output parts. --browser-check concatenates the
check head, HTML declarations, check input, shared HTML parser and check output.
Both modes compose original Zero source without an external parser or runtime.

Source files are limited to 524288 bytes and include nesting to 16 buffers.
Output is bounded to 524288 bytes. Files are checked for premature EOF, excess
bytes and size changes while reading. The compositor preserves source lines
and inserts a final newline when a fragment lacks one. It does not rewrite
string literals or program statements. Inputs must not be edited concurrently.

The output path is created exclusively after complete composition succeeds.
It is flushed and closed before reporting success. An existing output is
preserved; a failed new write removes only its own partial file. Source inputs
are read-only. There is no automatic replacement of the canonical kernel.

Use a composed kernel directly with the native builder:
  zero-integrate . kernel.next.zero
  zero-build . new-build kernel.next.zero

These commands use our own native Zero implementations. They do not launch
shells, subprocess compilers, source-transformer scripts or foreign runtimes.
